      "id": 1,
      "text": "Culbertson was founded in 1873 as a trading post and became Hitchcock County's first seat.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culbertson%2C_Nebraska"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village was named for Alexander Culbertson, an Indian agent who helped negotiate treaties.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culbertson%2C_Nebraska"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Culbertson’s historic Stone Church still stands, a reminder of the town’s early 20th‑century architecture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.nebraskatravelerguide.com/places/hitchcock/culbertson/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Twin River Walking Trail follows Frenchman Creek through shaded trees, offering a scenic stroll along the river.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.nebraskatravelerguide.com/places/hitchcock/culbertson/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Culbertson sits north of where Frenchman Creek meets the Republican River, a notable geographic marker.",
